### Real-Time Peak Detection in 1000 Hz Sensor Streams

When a Formula car navigates curbs or sudden braking zones, vertical accelerometers report spikes over 20G. A naive window maximum takes O(k) per sample, causing buffer bloat. Using a monotonic double-ended queue (`std::deque`), we track the sliding window maximum in amortized O(1) time.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <deque>

std::vector<float> slidingWindowPeak(const std::vector<float>& telemetry, int k) {
    std::deque<int> dq;
    std::vector<float> peaks;

    for (int i = 0; i < (int)telemetry.size(); ++i) {
        // Pop elements outside the current window of size k
        if (!dq.empty() && dq.front() <= i - k) {
            dq.pop_front();
        }
        // Maintain monotonic decreasing order in the deque
        while (!dq.empty() && telemetry[dq.back()] <= telemetry[i]) {
            dq.pop_back();
        }
        dq.push_back(i);
        if (i >= k - 1) {
            peaks.push_back(telemetry[dq.front()]);
        }
    }
    return peaks;
}
```

### Complexity Benchmark
- Space Complexity: O(k) auxiliary memory
- Time Complexity: O(N) linear scan over 50,000 telemetry points per stint.
